format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

lesson dose-and-time
title: All at once or over time
minutes: 5
xp: 50
teaches: u4-large-fast
teaches: u4-small-spread
relies_on: u4-cell-repair

description:
    The same amount of radiation can mean very different things depending on how fast it arrives. You will compare a big, fast dose with a small one spread over time.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation

supports:
    The threshold for acute radiation syndrome is about 1 sievert; effects are more severe at higher doses and dose rates; a low dose or one spread over a long time carries less risk because repair is more likely.

cite ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

supports:
    A very high exposure over a short time can cause nausea and vomiting within hours; low levels can add a small increase in the risk of cancer over a lifetime; about 99 percent of people given 100 millisieverts would not get cancer as a result.

card c01
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l3-predict-same-total
scored: 0

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Fast or slow
figure: fast-or-slow
figure_at: 1

body:
    How fast a dose arrives is called the [[dose-rate|dose rate]]. It is given in units such as microsieverts per hour or millisieverts per year. The same amount can arrive in minutes or over many years.

card c03
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l3-choice-doserate
connected: true

card c04
kind: teach
type: reveal
scored: 0
title: Your guess, answered
predicts: c01
figure: big-fast-dose
figure_at: 1

body:
    A very high dose in a short time is the dangerous case. The same total spread over a long time is much less risky, because the cells have time to repair damage in between. Effects are more severe at higher doses and higher dose rates.

